{
  "index": 60,
  "text": "A few patients had to be excluded due to insufficient quality of the tomographic images and some were excluded due to unexplained hip pain. Several attempts have been made to radiographically define and classify osteolysis (6\u20138, 10, 11) into a more biological perspective, i.e., with the scope of separating more aggressive lysis from indolent types. For this study, the hypothesis was however that volume of the lesion correlated to its hypothetically systemic effect. Of course, we have no evidence to support this idea and can only speculate on the lesions\u2019 biological activity.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 61,
  "text": "From an implant perspective, all articulations had 28 mm cobalt-chrome heads and a well-documented (43) uncemented titanium-alloy cup (Trilogy\u00ae, Zimmer/Biomet) After 2005, all acetabular liners were highly crosslinked polyethylene; coincidentally, all cases and 57% of the controls had a conventional (non-crosslinked) liner. We do not know how many acetabular components with osteolysis had empty screw-holes. The femoral trunnion may also have contributed metallic debris to the inflammatory reaction. In fact, several Bi-Metric\u00ae (Biomet) stems in the cohort are linked to severe trunnionosis (44).",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 12,
  "text": "The aim of this study was to investigate whether THA patients with asymptomatic periacetabular osteolysis, determined by CT, have an increased long-term risk of CVD compared to THA patients without osteolysis, and to assess time to the event.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 8,
  "text": "Osteolysis and aseptic loosening are the most common reasons for total hip arthroplasty (THA) revision surgery (12\u201314). The result might be extensive revision surgery (13). Revision surgery due to aseptic loosening/osteolysis is correlated with worse outcomes compared to primary surgery (2, 9). We have previously shown an increased long-term risk of cardiovascular-related mortality in patients treated with THA (15) and an increased relative risk of cerebrovascular events (CVE) among patients with osteoarthritis who received THA and later underwent revision surgery due to loosening of the prosthesis (16). Similarly, cardiovascular mortality rate after total knee arthroplasty exceeds that in the general population after 10 years (17).",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 9,
  "text": "Inflammatory processes are currently considered as central to the development and complications of CVD (18\u201320). Numerous biomarkers involved at various levels (21) of the inflammation cascade have been shown to be associated with adverse cardiovascular outcomes (22). Leukocytes have a significant role in the inflammatory process and the number of leucocytes is recognized as an inflammatory marker and predictor of cardiovascular events (21).",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 28,
  "text": "The outcome variables were diagnoses indicating CVD including cardiovascular events (CVE) such as acute myocardial infarction and development of atherosclerotic disease, according to ICD-9 and \u221210 codes (Appendix), and the time in years to CVD onset after surgery. Participants with outcome events were censored after CVD onset to avoid similar events appearing several times.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

